## What was wrong

Deleting a word froze the app because of a write-amplification bug in `SavedTermsStore`, not in the `save()` function you pasted — that function was just being *called hundreds of times per delete*.

`terms` persists on every mutation:
```swift
var terms: [SavedTerm] = [] {
    didSet { save() }   // full JSON encode + UserDefaults write
}
```

The old delete path did this:
```swift
func remove(_ term: SavedTerm) {
    terms.removeAll { $0.id == term.id }   // 1 save
    updateSortOrders()                     // ← N more saves
}

private func updateSortOrders() {
    for (index, _) in terms.enumerated() {
        terms[index].sortOrder = index     // each write fires didSet → save()
    }
}
```

Every `terms[index] = …` triggers `didSet`, which re-encodes the **entire** array and writes it to `UserDefaults` synchronously on the main thread. So one delete from an N-word list does ~N full-array encodes — **O(N²) main-thread work**. With a small list it's just lag; with a large imported vocabulary it blocks the main thread long enough that the OS watchdog kills the process (the SIGTERM you saw).

## The fix

`SwiftMandarin/Models/SavedTerm.swift` — each bulk mutation now produces a new array and assigns `terms` exactly once, so `save()` runs a single time:

```swift
func remove(_ term: SavedTerm) {
    terms = reindexed(terms.filter { $0.id != term.id })
}

private func reindexed(_ source: [SavedTerm]) -> [SavedTerm] {
    var result = source
    for index in result.indices { result[index].sortOrder = index }
    return result   // caller assigns to `terms` once → one save
}
```

`remove(at:)`, `remove(chinese:)`, and `move(from:to:)` got the same single-assignment treatment (they shared the old `updateSortOrders()` storm). A delete now performs **one** save instead of N+1, eliminating the freeze.

Verified: clean iOS Simulator Debug build succeeds with no errors or warnings.

One thing worth flagging for a follow-up (out of scope here): the same `didSet { save() }` per-mutation pattern exists in the sibling stores — `TranslationHistory` and `WorkbookBank` both call `remove(atOffsets:)`/`move` the same way. They don't have a reindex loop so they aren't O(N²), but if you ever see lag deleting from history or the workbook bank, that's the place to look.
